design_plan:
No lead plan is provided for this task — you are the lead as well as the builder. Using the Design section above and the screener record, design the task yourself and WRITE YOUR PLAN in `notes.md` BEFORE you build:
  - choose the main lever and at least one more, each with a DIFFERENT reason a solver would dismiss or skip it. The main lever must be HOW-type (its fix needs the mechanism understood: a producer the check never calls, a coupled must-keep-working value, a shape a plausible check mishandles, a route where the protection never existed); another entry point or a planted copy is only an extra;
  - decide the sites, routes and input shapes the security tests will cover;
  - identify the coupled must-keep-working behaviour next to each lever, for the functional tests;
  - for the injected track: decide the realistic sibling gap(s) to add — each must be code a maintainer could plausibly have left, reachable through the real entry point, covered by the task's one general property (never by a sentence of its own), and placed where the published fix never touches. First list EVERY possible sibling site and route you can find (the screener's O8/O8b leads plus your own search of the code). Then pick 2–4 of them: the most complex and hardest to find of all the candidates (how-type over where-type, far from where the report leads, with a plausible reason to dismiss), each with a different reason a solver would skip it. Record the full candidate list, and why each pick beat the others, in notes.md;
  - write the security property ONCE, in general terms over the protected data or outcome, before choosing routes. Every route and sibling must fall under it as written; the report must not describe where any of them lives;
  - if this candidate cannot support a fair task that meets the bar (flaw does not reproduce at the pinned commit through a real entry point, needs network/GPU/GUI at run time, or no design meets the bar), STOP and report why. Do not ship a weak task.
Follow every rule in the Design, Report, Tests and Proof sections exactly. You choose the design; the rules are fixed.
